87,1124,37018,90,23,15170,17,22,532,59,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIf $a$ and $b$ are real numbers, $a^2b^3=\\frac{32}{27}$, and $\\frac{a}{b^3}=\\frac{27}{4}$, what is $a+b$?\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve for \\(a + b\\) given the equations \\(a^2b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\) and \\(\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4}\\), we will proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the second equation in a more convenient form:\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4} \\implies a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\) into the first equation \\(a^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} b^3 \\right)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} \\right)^2 (b^3)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{729}{16} \\right) b^6 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(b^6\\), multiply both sides by \\(\\frac{16}{729}\\):\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{32}{27} \\cdot \\frac{16}{729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{32 \\cdot 16}{27 \\cdot 729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[\nb^6 = \\left( \\frac{8}{27} \\right)^2\n\\]\n\nTaking the sixth root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nb = \\pm \\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6da255c691219b743b7f5606613fe7111918804b991aeac691155327179f33d7"},"seconds":24.92737913131714,"seed":1113322538035828355,"stop":"eos","text":"Taking the sixth root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nb = \\pm \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute back \\(b = \\frac{2}{3}\\) and \\(b = -\\frac{2}{3}\\) into the expression for \\(a\\).\n\nFor \\(b = \\frac{2}{3}\\):\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^3 = \\frac{27}{4} \\cdot \\frac{8}{27} = 2\n\\]\n\nFor \\(b = -\\frac{2}{3}\\):\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\left( -\\frac{2}{3} \\right)^3 = \\frac{27}{4} \\cdot -\\frac{8}{27} = -2\n\\]\n\nNow, we find \\(a + b\\) for each case:\n\nFor \\(b = \\frac{2}{3}\\) and \\(a = 2\\):\n\\[\na + b = 2 + \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{6}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(b = -\\frac{2}{3}\\) and \\(a = -2\\):\n\\[\na + b = -2 - \\frac{2}{3} = -\\frac{6}{3} - \\frac{2}{3} = -\\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\nSince the problem does not specify which solution to choose, we consider both. However, typically, problems like this expect the positive solution unless contextually indicated otherwise. Here, we will box the positive solution:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{8}{3}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{8}{3}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Taking the sixth root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nb = \\pm \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute back \\(b = \\frac{2}{3}\\) and \\(b = -\\frac{2}{3}\\) into the expression for \\(a\\).\n\nFor \\(b = \\frac{2}{3}\\):\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^3 = \\frac{27}{4} \\cdot \\frac{8}{27} = 2\n\\]\n\nFor \\(b = -\\frac{2}{3}\\):\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\left( -\\frac{2}{3} \\right)^3 = \\frac{27}{4} \\cdot -\\frac{8}{27} = -2\n\\]\n\nNow, we find \\(a + b\\) for each case:\n\nFor \\(b = \\frac{2}{3}\\) and \\(a = 2\\):\n\\[\na + b = 2 + \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{6}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(b = -\\frac{2}{3}\\) and \\(a = -2\\):\n\\[\na + b = -2 - \\frac{2}{3} = -\\frac{6}{3} - \\frac{2}{3} = -\\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\nSince the problem does not specify which solution to choose, we consider both. However, typically, problems like this expect the positive solution unless contextually indicated otherwise. Here, we will box the positive solution:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{8}{3}}\n\\]","id":"algebra:912"}
